Ambient draft: mimetypes
$Id: mimetypes.readme,v 1.2 2005/07/04 23:06:09 laire Exp $


Introduction:
-------------
Mimetypes are a way to determine what an URI actually is. If it's a file its
type needs to be recognized. Same case for eg. an HTTP content.


Basics:
-------
- Identification: a mimetype can be determined by the stream/file content if
it's not provided otherwise. There are basically 3 ways to determine a
mimetype:

  - by analyzing the content. This works best when the content has a mark at
  the beginning of the file. Unfortunately it can be an expensive operation if
  this is not the case

  - by having the mimetype as metadata. For example an HTTP server sends such
  info in the headers. Sometimes mimetypes are badly configured, the most
  obvious case are missing mimetypes so the file is seen as text/plain or
  binary

  - by checking the file extension. Eg. *.jpg. It is possible that the
  extension is wrong, but rare though.

- Priority: given those 3 kind of identification we can use them in different
  cases.

  - file extention: fast, pretty reliable
  - content: slow, very reliable
  - metadata: fast, reliable but not always available


Recognizers:
------------
- Built in: N/A

- Multimedia: N/A

- Datatypes: Ambient can ask the datatypes subsystem to recognize the data.
The returned GID can be used, like:
  GID_SYSTEM (directory, executables, libraries, devices, fonts, ..)
   -> hexview
  GID_TEXT (text files, .txt, ..)
   -> textview
  GID_DOCUMENT (no clue)
   -> boopsiview
  GID_SOUND (sound data)
   -> sound output
  GID_INSTRUMENT (probably some aiff instrument)
   -> boopsiview
  GID_MUSIC (probably some tracker files)
   -> boopsiview
  GID_PICTURE (image)
   -> imageview
  GID_ANIMATION (animations, rare stuff)
   -> boopsiview
  GID_MOVIE (movies, rare stuff)
   -> boopsiview


Actions:
--------
Actions specify what to do with a mimetype. There can be multiple actions per
mimetypes. The first one is the default action, the next ones appear in the
context menu. The action types are:

 - external: run an external program, placeholder to specify arguments
 - view: use a view (only possible if there's a view that can handle it)
 - datatypes: use datatypes, always available as there's no way to find out if
   a datatype handles something
 - multimedia: use multimedia. available if there's a matching mimetype
 - internal: use built-in support if there's a matching datatype (eg.
   ogg-vorbis, script or mp3)


Storage:
--------
There are 2 databases. An internal database and the user database.


Updates:
--------
The user database is updated/populated by mossys:ambient/modules/mimedb.amod
whose version is checked against the one stored in the prefs. If the version
is newer, some mimedb function is called which updates the mime database. It's
also possible to trigger that function through the prefs (eg. 'rebuild
database', or even 'revert to defaults').

- Internal mimetypes (within Ambient):
  - ELF PPC
  - HUNK 68k

- External default mimetypes (in mimedb.amod)
  - scripts
  - ogg vorbis
  - mp3
  - multimedia
  - datatypes




